Wikipedia reserve table
html_table() returned eight columns, but four had the same displayed heading, “Foreign exchange reserves,” and the first two body rows repeated the multi-row header. I therefore renamed by position, removed those two rows, stripped footnote markers and Unicode spaces, parsed numeric fields, and converted the report date with lubridate::dmy(). I also removed any Total or World record, although neither appeared in this version. The cleaned file has 196 countries. Wikipedia contains one impossible date, 31 Apr 2024 for Albania; I kept it as missing instead of silently inventing a date.


Code
reserves_raw &lt;- read_html(reserves_url) |&gt;
  html_elements("table.wikitable") |&gt;
  purrr::pluck(1) |&gt;
  html_table(fill = TRUE)

names(reserves_raw) &lt;- c(
  "country", "continent", "reserves_with_gold_usd_millions",
  "change_with_gold_usd_millions", "reserves_without_gold_usd_millions",
  "change_without_gold_usd_millions", "last_reported_date", "reference"
)

reserves &lt;- reserves_raw |&gt;
  slice(-(1:2)) |&gt;
  mutate(across(everything(), clean_text)) |&gt;
  mutate(
    country = str_remove_all(country, "\\[[^]]+\\]") |&gt; str_squish(),
    across(ends_with("usd_millions"), parse_reserve),
    last_reported_date = suppressWarnings(lubridate::dmy(last_reported_date))
  ) |&gt;
  filter(!country %in% c("", "Total", "World")) |&gt;
  select(-reference)


I next changed the target to the Wikipedia population table. The reserve cleaner failed immediately because it asserted eight columns and deleted two supposed header rows; the population table has six columns and begins with data. Its fields also require different operations: % of world contains percent signs, the first record is World, and citations occur in the source and notes columns. This is exactly the silent-fragility problem when a scraper assumes layout rather than meaning. After a target-specific cleaner, the second dataframe has 239 locations after removing World.


Code
population_raw &lt;- read_html(population_url) |&gt;
  html_element("table.wikitable") |&gt;
  html_table(fill = TRUE)

population &lt;- population_raw |&gt;
  rename(
    location = 1, population = 2, world_share = 3,
    estimate_date = 4, source = 5, notes = 6
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(across(everything(), clean_text)) |&gt;
  mutate(
    location = str_remove_all(location, "\\[[^]]+\\]") |&gt;
      str_squish(),
    population = parse_number(population),
    world_share_pct = parse_number(world_share),
    estimate_date = suppressWarnings(lubridate::dmy(estimate_date))
  ) |&gt;
  filter(location != "World") |&gt;
  select(
    location, population, world_share_pct,
    estimate_date, source, notes
  )
